we've heard a lot about this year about  special prosecutors independent counsels  and congressional investigations but we  don't hear so much about inspectors  general who play a huge and critical  important role in the oversight of your  government there are 72 inspectors  general and they have an enormous staff  of about 13,500 employees joining me now  to discuss his role in duties is Michael  missile from the Department of Veterans  Affairs where he is the inspector  general director inspector general I  want to thank you first of all the  community as a whole I you know we  people I think do think all we need a  special prosecutor particularly when it  relates to the FBI and the Department of  Justice but behind the scenes we already  have embedded into the system people are  essentially their auditors right  what can they look at what can they not  look at and what do we expect the 850  people you employ at the Veterans  Administration to do on a daily basis so  what our job is is do oversight of the  programs and operations of the agency we  do that by conducting audits inspections  reviews and criminal investigations and  what we're really looking for is fraud  waste abuse and inefficiencies so we use  our powers to get whatever information  we want from the agency to talk to  whatever person that they have to  respond to you don't come in with the  Inspector General badge and say I'm here  to talk to you  I need those documents do they have to  give them to you they have to give them  to us absolutely and they do the only  thing that they can't do though is once  they leave and they depart then you  you're you don't have the ability to  reach out and go touch the get that  information we can still subpoena them  for information documents but correct we  do not have the ability to subpoena a  former employee to talk to us so just to  give you hom a sense at the VA you go to  the website by the way if you go to at  oversight gov there's the Twitter handle  it's brand-new with all these  investigations all these reports about  what's going on in your government  oversight gov is also a website that the  viewers at home can go to but just at  the VA you issued 181 reports eleven  hundred and forty-four recommendations  and had a 4.2 billion dollar impact  what you're looking at the VA I mean we  all care about veterans that have served  this nation it seems like a disaster and  a mess where is the VA at today and what  are your biggest concerns we're working  very hard to fix whatever issues we find  we have a hotline that gets 40,000  contacts a year although we triage every  one of those contacts we hear from  veterans we hear from VA employees we  heard from whistleblowers were really  critical to but what's not working well  at the VA today I mean there has been  bills that have been passed in this  Congress President Trump has made it a  priority but today as the Inspector  General looking under the hood what is  your biggest concern at the VA we're  just making sure that the programs  workers are designed and that the money  is spent as appropriated so we've looked  as you pointed out we made a number of  recommendations and hopefully that means  those programs are going to be improved  and then we're always finding other  things we look very hard we have a very  proud staff that works every day to help  make the programs and operations better  for veterans waste fraud and abuse we  all talk about every year people talk  about wanting to root out the waste  fraud and abuse have you've been able to  pin a number on that how big is the  waste fraud abuse it's extremely large  like is that hundreds of dollars is it  tens of billions of dollars and the  billions of dollars just in the last six  months our economic impact was over ten  billion dollars and that's in cost  savings that's in recoveries we had and  other ways we measure economic impact so  what's the number one thing you want  people to know about inspectors general  because particularly over at the  Department of Justice a lot of calls for  a special prosecutor but do you really  need a special prosecutor if you have an  Inspector General like Michael Horowitz  doing their job over there I think one  of the differentiating features about an  Inspector General is our independence we  don't report to anybody we don't report  to the head of the agency we don't  report to Congress so this allows us to  do our job the way we think the best way  possible  it allows us to call it like we see it  to do our job in a fair and objective  manner and to get things right well look  I can tell you as the former chairman of  the Oversight Committee I relied on the  inspectors general across the government  they these people will go dive in for  a year or two and our worry is that they  find this good information and that it  doesn't actually get implemented by an  administration or that Congress forgets  about it when legislations happening so  to the 13,500 inspectors generals the  staff and the 850 people that work for  you at the Veterans Administration  Godspeed we appreciate all that you do  and and looking after our taxpayer  dollars thank you and working hard every  day thank you
 

